WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.020
Dusty Diamond's All-Star Softball.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:06.060
Choose your team and play ball.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.520
Dusty Diamond's All-Star Softball.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.620
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:30.720
I'm Mike.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:32.040
I'm Sean.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.220
And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:38.400
And the headline is choose your team because that'll take a while in this game.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.640
Yeah, it's like an actual like schoolyard draft.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.100
Is Dusty Diamond in there anywhere?

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:45.880
See, now that's a good question.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.700
Is Dusty Diamond a famous...

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.600
Mike, you know more about this stuff.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.700
Is Dusty Diamond like in the Hall of Fame?

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.480
Of softball.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:01.460
Do you assume that Dusty Diamond was the name of a person and not just referring to how dusty these diamonds are?

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.740
Oh, so you're saying that maybe it's a play on words, Joe?

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:06.580
No, not even a play on words.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.020
I just think it's literally a baseball diamond that's dusty because it's dry dirt.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.740
Well, you can get it... I highly doubt that.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.060
You can get advice from Dusty Diamond.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:16.780
Oh, can you? I mean, I thought that...

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:21.700
You can, and I don't know if we're gonna go with the bit or not, but he's not a real person.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.500
Hmm. I started answering and then I stopped because I realized I might not know what I'm talking about.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.580
That's why I cut off there.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.420
Because it's not the dusty...

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.480
Like, Dusty Diamond is not like the field you're playing on.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.240
It is Dusty Diamond's like possessive all-star softball.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.900
Like as if it was Ken Griffey Jr.'s Major League Baseball.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.200
That's true. I'm seeing the apostrophe now.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.860
Right, right. Or Dusty's Diamond's like all these diamonds are just all-star softball.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.900
Like, it could have just been like that.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.040
But it's...

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.500
But it is... It's not.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.880
It's not. Yeah, it's not. Right, right.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.300
Okay, I don't know how much longer we should go on about that.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.280
Maybe we should talk about the game or something.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.280
Actually, no, let's start somewhere more interesting.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.980
We've played plenty of baseball games, so we don't really need to get into it right away.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:10.160
But this is, at least for me, my first ever softball video game.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.100
And it made me realize, I don't really know a lot about the differences between baseball and softball.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.880
I know that the... It's a different ball.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:18.940
It's a bigger ball.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.720
But why do you think they went with softball instead of baseball?

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:29.820
I think they wanted to make it feel more, at least for the slow pitch, more recreational.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.980
You know, because you're not playing...

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.320
Well, there are professional diamonds, but you also play like on a cliff or on like an island

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.880
or in like a schoolyard or something.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:39.840
You know, a lot of like...

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.420
A lot of like...

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:43.180
It feels like your kids playing softball.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:50.720
Yeah, like you were mentioning before we got off track, like it does feel more like a...

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.720
A proto-backyard baseball or whatever that franchise is called.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.900
Where it's not really taking itself too seriously.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.860
And, I mean, that's not to say that softball isn't as serious a sport.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.400
But, I guess it doesn't have the same reputation.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:20.620
Well, no, and also, you know, softball, there's serious softball players, serious sport, but then also a lot of people who want to play like in a beer league play beer league softball, not beer league baseball.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:31.040
So, like, yes, there's serious softball, but I think there's more recreational softball for, you know, grown men who are dads than there are recreational baseball.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:32.980
Like American soccer.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:38.820
Right. And even like half the playing fields, that's like a weird...

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:43.120
Half of the baseball diamonds that you can play on are not even like stadiums.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.260
They are just like people's backyards and stuff.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.740
So, yeah, they're totally going for that recreational feel.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.920
Including, I believe that some of them have like their own...

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.240
Like the field is different, right?

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.640
There's like some obstacles.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.260
Yeah, they have their own rules.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.320
Like, I like that.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:06.160
Yeah, this is where I started to realize, like, I wonder if I don't know the differences between softball rules and baseball rules.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.260
Because some of the like, oh, this field's rule is that like if a foul ball pop-up is caught, it's an out.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:12.840
I'm like, isn't that just a regular rule?

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.620
It's always a rule in baseball, right?

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.320
I thought so too.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.760
Unless it's not the case in softball.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.100
The wording was weird there.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.460
Yeah. But like, were there fields where that wasn't true?

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:27.300
Where you would catch a foul ball, it's not an out?

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.880
It's always an out.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.560
Unless it's like one of those Savannah Banana games, right, Joe?

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.860
Yeah. Where like all of a sudden if a fan catches it, it's also an out.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.600
Highly recommend Savannah Bananas, by the way, folks.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:47.020
But, okay, so like other than the, you know, adding some obstacles to a, you know, like a field is whatever.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:56.800
There are some other interesting ones though too, where like, if you hit it past the home run line, it doesn't, like it doesn't mean that you can keep going further.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:05.140
I like that. So like technically inside the park home runs are easier to achieve in, in park field.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:08.260
Oh, you mean if it, if it like bounces past the, the run line?

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.320
Yeah. Right. There's a whole, there's no field, there's no fence there.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.140
So instead in, um, which one is it?

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:23.040
Park field. Um, but then there's also like, it's like a, there's a pool in that one, like over by the third bay side or something, or lake.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.200
Uh, so anyway, the point being, forget about, uh, all the chit chat.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:41.000
The parks are what make this game something a little bit more unique on top of the fact that, uh, you know, overall the feel is this is not a professional game of baseball being simulated.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:48.060
This is similar to, uh, what our generation would describe as like the backyard baseball style games, right?

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.360
Where all of a sudden it was like kid Derek Jeter versus kid Alex Rodriguez.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:58.340
This is like, you have a roster of an insane amount of people to pick from.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:07.040
And you started on a screen where you have all of their faces and you just, I don't know, like what, what was the strategy there for you guys?

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.160
First time you're booting up the game.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.260
Like, how are you picking your team?

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.980
I just had to pick the weirdest looking people.

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:22.420
Like whoever was wearing sunglasses or whoever kind of like a vampire or if they had like purple skin or what the closest thing to it.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.400
I just wanted a, I just wanted a team of freaks.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:42.960
And I think, you know, like if you're, it's the equivalent of if, if you're lining up for, you know, gym, gym class softball or after school softball, and you're doing a draft, like, you know, I would, I would pick the people that I thought were the most, uh,

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:44.920
athletic among us.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.000
But in lieu of that weird.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:56.320
Yeah. Well, I, I went a similar route where I just like picked people that I thought looked like would, would be like odd couples.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.580
You know, I wanted like a ragtag group of people you'd never expect to see together.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.380
So like some cool guys with some strange guys, I put them next to each other.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.020
Like, Oh, these guys are gonna be buddies by the end.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:13.280
Um, but the way that I would probably do this if I, uh, played more having read the manual now is you realize there's actually a lot of stats.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.400
Everybody has like stats.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:16.740
Yeah. I don't get time for that.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:17.660
Get that from the manual.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.360
So like, that's how you can build your team.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.920
That's how it's obvious. I think meant to, to be, but yeah, I just had fun with it also.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:29.480
Yeah. With 60 players that is asking a bit much, but does add a ton of replay value.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:40.320
I suppose, uh, the bigger deal about the individual characters, other than that, some do share, even if you're doing Sean strategy, like some of the faces do look awfully similar.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:46.340
Uh, there's a couple of those vampire looking dudes, uh, or devil dude, you have a whole team of them.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:58.300
Yeah. Uh, so, so if you do just pick randomly, you might miss out on some of the, uh, like special abilities of certain characters that also help depending on which stadium you're playing at.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:06.040
Yeah. So again, like some have fences, so some players can jump over those fences or sorry, climb on them.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:17.060
Uh, and that, that gives a distinct advantage over somebody who say, uh, can swim, which apparently not every, uh, able-bodied person here can do.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:21.540
And they can go into the water hazards and, uh, catch the outs that way.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:26.340
Yeah. The first time I saw one of these special abilities happen, I thought it was a bug.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:34.400
Like I, there's, there's like some characters that can fly or at least like jump really high and then sort of hold the jump.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:40.060
And I'm like, did, did something just, did this like sprite just become unmoored from the game?

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:42.620
And then I realized like, oh no, that's like a feature.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.940
Yeah. The, uh, they float in the midair, uh, but these are like cool things.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:51.880
Oh no, there's a float one, but there's also a fly one, but the fly one's interesting because they can fly only at night.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.020
Has to be a night game.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.340
Uh, vampires.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:09.200
Yeah. And there's also increased power at night too, which for a different thing, which I think is, um, I think all of these things are the kind of, uh, things that would bother me if they were in a game of, uh, major league baseball.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:14.540
But since they're here in dusty diamonds, all-star softball, uh, I don't know.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:25.440
It really brought out, um, uh, a, a more carefree and more like what, like I didn't stress when I was striking out or whatever, more than I would in the usual NES baseball games.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:35.280
Cause like, you know, it's, it's hard to, to just constantly keep swinging at pitches that are balls and you, and you know, you missed it, but you swing anyway because you don't have a strategy.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.380
So you just swing at every, uh, pitch that the pitcher throws.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.340
And that gets frustrating in a game where you're supposed to be playing as like the New York Mets.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:43.460
And I love the New York Mets.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.020
So I gotta, you know, I gotta root for my Mets and win games as the New York Mets.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:10:02.820
But when I'm just like a ragtag group of kids that I just picked and you know, uh, the grass fields have like, they make it harder to run in or, um, or, you know, like I, I didn't know that I was supposed to like have this character for this.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:07.100
Cause I found it to be, uh, you know, a lot less stressful knowing that.

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:09.800
Yeah. It was a very, uh, it was chill.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.500
It wasn't like the, the gameplay itself wasn't too complex or anything.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:15.520
And I kinda, I kinda enjoyed that from a baseball game.

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.140
It was just the, it was simple.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:24.960
Yeah. I think that, you know, the, those, those powers are, are nice and they, they add some flavor.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:39.960
Um, the, I guess like just the, the, how infrequently you use them contributed a bit to where, contribute a bit to the feeling that I have is that they didn't really go far enough with the goofball antics.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:45.040
Like I appreciated that, like some of them, you know, they're, they're swinging mops there.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:51.280
They're swinging like old, uh, like clubs from caveman days.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.220
And, uh, that that's all very cool too.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:11:02.760
But it, it, most of the time it, it just kind of felt like we were playing almost every other baseball game that we played.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:09.700
Like it didn't, like the core gameplay didn't stray too far away from what has come before.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.900
And I think that was a little bit of a missed opportunity.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:17.600
Yeah. But it was, it was a testament to the fact that like, I could just pick this up.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:18.480
I didn't have to look at a man.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:20.580
I mean, I could pick it up and I know exactly how to play immediately.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:27.080
Right. I think a, I think a lesser game would have made like the teams, like, this is the cavemen.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:32.460
Right. And like, they're all, they all use the club and they all have like this one special ability.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:34.300
And then another one's like the vampires.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:41.840
Right. It's like, it was nice that you kind of get this amazing cast of characters to which the

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:45.200
point where like the manual even has bios for all of them.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.540
And they're not even like relevant to like, yeah, they tell you about like a little bit about their stats or whatever.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:54.040
There's a separate thing for that, but these are like bios about the characters themselves.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:59.700
So if you kind of create your own mythos around like, you know, uh, Sid Savage, right?

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:01.460
Like that guy always comes in clutch for you.

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:05.320
And then you can like read up on them and get a little bit of backstory there on Sid.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:07.880
Uh, I think that's fun.

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:10.240
And I think that that's what makes this game.

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:17.740
The most unique thing about it is that playground aspect to it of picking, you know, your random

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:19.020
team. It's gotta be random.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:23.320
There's no way I'm sure there's competitive dusty diamonds, all-stars softball somewhere

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.000
in the world, but it's not for me.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:29.900
And anytime I boot up this game, you better believe I'm just picking based on like Sean

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:32.280
said, the faces and I'm just going with that.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:34.480
That's my team. That's also kind of what I mean.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:40.160
Like the most of what makes it cool is sort of outside the mechanics of the game.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:47.500
It's, it's more so like the fact that each of these goofballs has a bio in the manual.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.380
That's not really reflected in the game itself.

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:54.740
I think it would have been cool to have a bit more integrated into the game.

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:56.280
The weirdness.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:05.280
Yeah. It is kind of like a quirky fun skin on top of the base baseball game.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.300
Softball game. Right.

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:12.160
Cause the game, cause to your point, Sean, the game is selling you on the weirdness factor,

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:16.360
or at least like, you know, that it's not like every other baseball game on NES you've

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:21.560
played and you're kind of saying, well, it kind of is actually like mechanically still

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:26.540
works like every NES baseball game I've played, which is mostly true.

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:30.540
Even just looking at, you know, not even thinking about the special rules of the parks or whatever,

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:33.740
but just the actual controls of the game and everything.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.680
Yeah. It does kind of, um, it doesn't do anything to stand out there.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:42.720
And so instead it focuses on, it highlights the weird factor.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:51.220
And in the one player mode, uh, you know, you can't just play exhibition matches or whatever,

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:59.300
but there is also sort of a, uh, not a story mode, but a five round elimination match thing

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:05.320
where like you keep taking on, um, prebuilt teams that eventually leads you to the final

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:11.660
championship match against the Amazons, uh, who apparently are like undefeated, at least

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:12.560
according to the manual.

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:17.760
Uh, do you think you need more than like a five?

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:22.220
Like, you know, we played games where you could simulate an entire 162 game season.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:29.420
Is five game elimination like enough to substantiate a, uh, story behind the game?

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:34.380
I guess it depends on what you want, but for me, I think it's plenty.

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:35.820
That's exactly what I want.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:38.980
I want five games and just to sit down for like an NES game.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:44.080
Like it depends, I guess, but like, I don't want to sit down and start a whole season of this.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:46.220
That makes it sound like I hate this game.

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:47.200
It's great. It's fun.

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:49.440
But like, if that's exactly what it is, it's fun.

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.480
It's quirky. It's like a chill game.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:53.980
So like five games, I think is, is plenty.

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:57.140
It's not like, you know, it's not like an MLB.

00:14:57.220 --> 00:15:00.960
It's not MLB the show where like, okay, I want to get like invested in this for, you know,

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:04.560
for like the whole summer or whatever, which I Joe don't want that either.

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:07.880
But I know a lot of people do want that, but I don't think this is trying to do that.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:14.420
Yeah. Kind of like, I know in a lot of other sports games, you could just like simulate the playoffs

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:17.800
or not simulate the playoffs, but like have there be a playoff bracket.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.940
You're not playing the entire season leading up to it.

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:28.300
Um, but I think that even that might be too much for some moods I might be in.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:36.460
What I, what I think I liken it to more is, um, back in the day I play jet moto, uh, which

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:41.180
is a fake jet ski, uh, future racing game.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:49.460
Um, but basically you could either just do individual races or you could make your own

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:56.380
like circuit where you'd go from X, uh, X course to Y course and you could do it in whatever

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:59.000
order you wanted. It could be however long you wanted.

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:04.560
And at the end you could have like total standings between all those races or I guess

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:08.620
like, you know, any cup in, uh, like Mario cart.

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:13.780
So I think that's a better, it's like, it's not bite size, but it's almost bite size.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:19.160
And even just to take it out of the genre for a second, you think about, um, arcade fighters

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:23.960
or, you know, something like street fighter, uh, you really in street fighter two, you

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:29.600
only fought like six or seven other people maximum before, you know, before you beat

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:32.520
the game technically, but that was a really hard feat to do.

